   "notes": "Realistic perception: under each sink the hot and cold stops share the look-alike group water_valve (\"water shutoff valve\" until inspected), the hidden _leak and its component are never glance-visible, and an active leak names its component only to a close inspection (inspect where=closely); a plain inspect gives only the severity. Variants (seed % 3): kitchen cold-stop compression fitting dripping into the sink base (hidden_leak kitchen_supply, a puddle downhill) with an old dry crust on the hot stop; a bathroom P-trap slip nut that leaks only while water drains (hidden_leak bath_trap, no earlier evidence) with an old crust on the cold stop - found only by running the tap and looking at the trap; the kitchen hot stop leaking with the crust on the cold stop. The report must name the leaking component (the value the close look showed) to the adult; reports on the decoy crust, the cabinets or dry joints fail; taps must be off at the end. Wrong plans: report the wet-looking cabinet (glance); pick a look-alike stop after a plain look (severity, not the component); per variant, take the old dry crust for the leak, never run water (the drain-side leak stays hidden) or check only the kitchen."
